.TH HOSTASHI 1 "" "Hostashi" "Hostashi Manual"

.SH NAME
hostashi \- hosting control panel for a single Linux server


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBhostashi\fP \fIcommand\fP [\fIflags\fP] [\fIarguments\fP]


.SH DESCRIPTION
Hostashi runs websites with nginx, PHP\-FPM, Node.js, Python and MariaDB or
PostgreSQL on one Debian or Ubuntu server. The same binary is the
unprivileged web process (\fBserve\fP), the root agent that performs typed
operations (\fBagent\fP), the helpers that systemd units start, and the
command line client described here.

.PP
Most commands talk to the panel's API and need \fBhostashi login\fP first,
also as root. Commands marked "root" in their description talk to the
agent directly and only work as root on the server. Run a command with
\fB\-h\fP for its flags.


.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBlogin [\-token] [\-name NAME] [URL]\fP
log in to the panel's API through the browser: prints a link and a code to approve in the panel, then stores an API token in ~/.config/hostashi/credentials.json; \-token reads an existing token from stdin instead. URL defaults to http://127.0.0.1:8080; HOSTASHI_URL and HOSTASHI_TOKEN override the stored login (ADR\-0058)

.TP
\fBlogout\fP
forget the stored token (revoke it in the panel if it leaked)

.TP
\fBwhoami\fP
show the account and panel the CLI talks to

.TP
\fBapi [AREA [COMMAND [ARGS...]]]\fP
call any operation of the panel's API with the stored login, e.g. hostashi api sites list or hostashi api sites get blog; without arguments it lists the areas, \-h explains a command

.TP
\fBserve\fP
run the web process (never as root)

.TP
\fBagent\fP
run the privileged agent (root only)

.TP
\fBping\fP
check that the agent answers

.TP
\fBdoctor [\-json]\fP
check the server and every site without changing anything (root on the server); exit code 3 if a check failed, 0 otherwise

.TP
\fBservice list [\-json] | service ID [SITE] ACTION\fP
show the managed services with their state and restarts, or start, stop, restart or reload one where the service catalog allows it, through the panel's API (needs hostashi login, ADR\-0052)

.TP
\fBsystem info | system timezones | system timezone ZONE | system hostname NAME | system reboot HOSTNAME\fP
show or change the server's time zone and hostname, or reboot it after typing its hostname, through the panel's API (needs hostashi login, ADR\-0060)

.TP
\fBsite create NAME DOMAIN[,DOMAIN...] [POOL] | site delete NAME | site list\fP
create a static site with its domains, delete a site with its data, or list the sites, through the panel's API (needs hostashi login)

.TP
\fBpool add [\-default] NAME PATH | pool remove NAME | pool list\fP
storage pools for site homes (ADR\-0016, root on the server): PATH is a mounted, empty ext4 or XFS volume with nosuid,nodev and an /etc/fstab entry; Hostashi never formats or mounts; \-default makes the new pool the default for new sites

.TP
\fBpool rename NAME NEWNAME | pool default NAME\fP
rename a pool (its path and sites stay) or make it the default for new sites, through the panel's API (needs hostashi login)

.TP
\fBsite files NAME\fP
start the file manager's socket of a site and print its path (ADR\-0040)

.TP
\fBsite limits NAME [preset=NAME | KEY=VALUE...]\fP
show a site's limits, give it a preset, or change single values through the panel's API (needs hostashi login, ADR\-0015); keys: memory, tasks, cpu\-priority (low, normal, high), cpu\-quota, disk, inodes, php\-workers, db\-size, db\-connections; 0 means no limit, values left out stay as they are

.TP
\fBweb apply\fP
read the complete web state as JSON from stdin and make it live, e.g. {"sites":[{"user":"blog","domains":["blog.example.com"]}]}

.TP
\fBdb list | db create SITE NAME | db delete NAME | db user create DATABASE USER | db user password USER | db user delete USER | db import DATABASE FILE | db export DATABASE FILE\fP
manage databases through the panel's API (needs hostashi login); FILE is relative to the site home and ends in .sql or .sql.gz; the password is read from stdin, an empty line lets the panel generate one

.TP
\fBbackup run\fP
one backup now (root; normally started by hostashi\-backup.service)

.TP
\fBbackup get | backup set | backup hostkey | backup start | backup status\fP
backup settings through the panel's API (needs hostashi login); set reads the settings as JSON and hostkey one public key from stdin; start runs a backup now

.TP
\fBbackup restore SITE SNAPSHOT | backup restore\-status ID\fP
restore a snapshot into a new directory restore\-<time> in the site's home, through the panel's API (needs hostashi login)

.TP
\fBbackup deleted [SITE] | backup restore\-deleted SITE SNAPSHOT TARGET\fP
list deleted sites that still have snapshots, or the snapshots of one; restore one into the existing site TARGET (admins)

.TP
\fBbackup delete\-deleted SITE | backup keep\-deleted SITE\fP
delete the backups of a deleted site with the next backup run, or keep them after all (admins)

.TP
\fBbackup restore\-run ID\fP
one restore (root; started by hostashi\-restore@ID.service)

.TP
\fBbackup key show\fP
print the repository password and the panel key to keep outside the server (root only, never available over the API)

.TP
\fBcloudpanel scan [\-root DIR] [\-json] [\-full]\fP
read a CloudPanel server (root on the old server, or a read\-only copy of its disk) and show which sites can be imported; changes nothing, hides snippets and cron commands unless \-full is given

.TP
\fBcloudpanel database\fP
make a new password for the read\-only database user on the old server and print the SQL to run there (shown once)

.TP
\fBcloudpanel import SITE REMOTE_USER DOCROOT [OLD_DB[:NEW_DB] ...] | cloudpanel import\-status ID\fP
copy the files of DOCROOT (below /home/REMOTE_USER/ on the old server) into SITE's home as cloudpanel\-import, and dump each old database into cloudpanel\-import\-db/NEW_DB\-<time>\&.sql for "db import", through the agent (root on the server); run it again for the final sync

.TP
\fBcloudpanel import\-run ID\fP
one import (root; started by hostashi\-import@ID.service)

.TP
\fBdbjob run ID | dbjob finish ID\fP
one import or export of a database on an external server and its cleanup (root; started by hostashi\-dbjob@ID.service)

.TP
\fBnode status | node install MAJOR\fP
the installed Node.js lines and the last installation, or start the installation of an LTS line, through the agent (root on the server, ADR\-0054)

.TP
\fBnode\-install MAJOR\fP
download, check and unpack the pinned Node.js build of MAJOR into /opt/hostashi/node/MAJOR (root; started by hostashi\-node\-install@MAJOR.service)

.TP
\fBwp\-run PHAR\fP
run the WordPress quick install with WP\-CLI from the job in the unit's credentials (site user; started by hostashi\-wp@SITE.service)

.TP
\fBwpcli\-install\fP
download and check the pinned WP\-CLI phar into /opt/hostashi/wp\-cli/VERSION (root; started by hostashi\-wpcli\-install.service, ADR\-0069)

.TP
\fBpython status | python install LINE | python remove LINE\fP
the installed Python lines and the last installation, start the installation of a line (for example 3.13) or remove one that no site uses, through the panel's API (ADR\-0061)

.TP
\fBpython\-install LINE\fP
download, check and unpack the pinned CPython build of LINE into /opt/hostashi/python/LINE (root; started by hostashi\-python\-install@LINE.service)

.TP
\fBgit\-sync \-url URL \-branch BRANCH \-cache DIR \-release DIR \-result FILE [flags]\fP
fetch a site's branch and write one commit into a new release directory (the site user, never root; started by hostashi\-deploy@SITE.service, ADR\-0062)

.TP
\fBuser recover [\-public\-url URL] NAME\fP
reset NAME's password and second factors, enable the account and print a new invitation link (root; the way back in when the last admin is locked out)

.TP
\fBfiles\-helper \-home DIR [\-idle DURATION]\fP
the file manager's helper for one site (the site user; started by hostashi\-files@SITE.socket, ADR\-0040)

.TP
\fBsetup\-token\fP
print the one\-time token for the first login (read as the serve user or root)

.TP
\fBcompletion bash|zsh|fish\fP
print the shell completion script; the installer puts it into each shell's completion directory below /usr/local/share

.TP
\fBversion [\-schema]\fP
print the version, or with \-schema the database schema version this binary migrates to (package scripts, ADR\-0050)


.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
HOSTASHI_URL
Address of the panel's API; overrides the stored login.

.TP
HOSTASHI_TOKEN
API token; overrides the stored login.

.TP
XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Directory of the stored login, by default \fI~/.config\fP\&.


.SH FILES
.TP
\fI~/.config/hostashi/credentials.json\fP
The panel address and API token that \fBhostashi login\fP stores (mode 0600).

.TP
\fI/usr/share/hostashi/install.sh\fP
The installer that sets up the server and runs again on every package upgrade.

.TP
\fI/etc/hostashi/install.options\fP
The options the installer ran with; upgrades reuse them.


.SH EXIT STATUS
\fB0\fP on success, \fB1\fP when the command failed, \fB2\fP for a usage error.
\fBhostashi doctor\fP exits with \fB3\fP when a check failed.


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBnginx\fP(8), \fBsystemctl\fP(1), \fBrestic\fP(1)

.PP
